const intro = [
  "Most households already know they should have an emergency water plan. Very few have one that survives contact with an actual emergency.",
  "The usual answers all fail in predictable ways. Bottled water expires, takes up a cupboard and runs out in about two days. Boiling needs power or fuel, which is often the thing you have lost. Purification tablets taste of chemicals, need half an hour of waiting, and children will refuse to drink the result. A large gravity system like a Berkey works well but costs $200 to $500 and lives on a kitchen counter, which is no help in the car or a go-bag.",
  "A personal filter solves a narrower problem properly: turning whatever water you can reach into water you can safely drink, with no power and no waiting. We compared the leading options on filtration, how easy they are to use under pressure, how well they suit a family rather than a lone hiker, and value. Here are the five worth owning in 2026, ranked by our <a href='/how-we-rate/'>Insider Score</a>.",
];

const highlights = [
  {
    title: "Three Ways To Drink",
    text: "Straight from the source, through the squeeze pouch, or screwed onto a normal water bottle — so it fits how your household actually behaves.",
  },
  {
    title: "Nothing To Set Up",
    text: "No pumping, no batteries, no chemicals and no waiting. Fill, squeeze, drink. That matters when nobody is thinking clearly.",
  },
  {
    title: "Small Enough To Forget",
    text: "Two ounces. It sits in a glove box or a drawer for years without being in the way, which is the only reason it will still be there when it counts.",
  },
];

const deepDive = {
  heading: "Why Survival Sip Is Our #1 Pick",
  body: [
    "A person lasts about three days without water. That is the whole reason water sits at the top of every emergency checklist, above food, above light, above heat. And it is why the gap between owning a filter and not owning one is not a small convenience — it is the difference between an inconvenient week and a genuine emergency.",
    "Almost every filter in this category was built for someone who chose to be out there. Survival Sip is the one built for a household that did not choose it, and that shows up in the detail that decides whether a filter actually gets used: how you get the water into a person.",
    "Compare it directly with our #2 pick. The LifeStraw is a genuinely good filter and it costs less, and we have ranked it second rather than buried it. But the basic model is a straw. Using it means getting face-down at the floodwater, the pond, the rain barrel. That is fine for an adult on a trail in daylight. Hand it to a frightened seven-year-old during a blackout and it is close to useless. Survival Sip gives you three routes to the same glass of water: drink at the source, squeeze from the pouch, or screw it onto a bottle already in your kitchen. Somebody in your house will manage one of those three without being taught.",
    "The manufacturer states a 0.1 micron hollow-fibre membrane removing 99.99% of bacteria and parasites — E. coli, Giardia and Cryptosporidium among them — and up to 1,800 gallons per filter. Those are their published figures, not our test results, and we have said so plainly. What we can judge for ourselves is the design, and it holds up: nothing to charge, nothing to expire, nothing to assemble, and 90 days to send it back if it does not convince you.",
    "Then there is the price, which is the part people talk themselves out of and should not. At its current 50% discount it costs less than a takeaway pizza, and unlike the pizza it sits in a drawer for years and does not expire. Buying one for each person in the house is not really a financial decision. It is a five-minute decision you either make this week or keep postponing until the week you cannot.",
    "The honest limitation, which applies to nearly everything on this list: it removes bacteria and parasites, not viruses. For a US power cut, a boil notice or a storm, that is exactly the right level of protection. If your real risk is drinking water overseas, that is a different problem and our #5 pick handles it — at roughly four times the price.",
  ],
};

const editorial = {
  heading: "The Preparedness Purchase People Actually Follow Through On",
  html: `
<p>There is a particular kind of guilt that shows up when a storm is on the news. You think about the torch you never replaced the batteries in, the water you meant to store, the plan you never quite made. Then the weather passes and the feeling goes with it, until the next time.</p>

<p>Most preparedness advice makes that worse rather than better. It starts with generators and shelving units and 55-gallon drums, and the sheer scale of it makes the whole thing easy to postpone. So people postpone it, over and over, and stay exactly where they started.</p>

<h3>Why water is the part that actually matters</h3>

<p>A person can last around three days without water. That number is the reason water sits at the top of every emergency list ahead of food, light and heat. It is also the part of preparedness with the shortest gap between "I have nothing" and "I am fine", which makes it the sensible place to begin.</p>

<p>The standard fix is to buy bottled water, and it half-works. A case in the garage covers a day or two for a family, and then it does not. It expires. It takes up room you wanted for something else. And in the situations where you most need it — a flood, an evacuation, a car that has to leave in ten minutes — it is heavy, bulky and staying exactly where it is.</p>

<div class="pull-note">A filter does not run out at the end of the case. It turns whatever water you can reach into water you can drink, over and over, for as long as the filter lasts.</div>

<h3>What we looked at</h3>

<p>Filtration first: pore size, and what the maker states it removes. Then how it is used, which we weighted more heavily than most reviews do, because a filter that is fiddly under pressure is a filter that fails at the exact moment it is needed. Then whether a household can use it, not just an experienced hiker. Then value, and what protection you have if it disappoints.</p>

<p>That last category is where the direct-sold products differ from the retail ones, and we will be straightforward about it: our top pick is sold through its own store, so there is no large independent review aggregate to check it against. We have scored it on published specifications, design and the guarantee behind it, and we have said so on the card rather than dressing an assessment up as data.</p>

<h3>Where we would send you elsewhere</h3>

<p>If price is the only thing that matters, the <a href="${AMZ("B07C56LR6N", "lifestraw_editorial")}" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" target="_blank"><strong>LifeStraw Personal</strong></a> at #2 filters well and costs very little. Just be clear about what you are buying: a straw, used by getting down to the water.</p>

<p>If you want the longest possible service life and do not mind maintenance, the <a href="${AMZ("B01CWFR92M", "sawyer_editorial")}" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" target="_blank"><strong>Sawyer Mini</strong></a> at #3 outlasts everything here, provided you keep backflushing it.</p>

<p>And if your real risk is drinking water abroad rather than at home, the <a href="${AMZ("B0C1JHC2M6", "grayl_editorial")}" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" target="_blank"><strong>Grayl GeoPress</strong></a> at #5 handles viruses, which nothing else on this list claims to do.</p>

<h3>Our verdict</h3>

<p>For the situation most US households are actually preparing for — the power goes out, the taps stop or a boil notice lands, and everyone in the house needs to drink — the <a href="${SIP_LINK}" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" target="_blank"><strong>Survival Sip</strong></a> is the one we would put in the drawer. It is small enough that it will still be there in three years, simple enough that anyone in the house can use it, and cheap enough that buying one for every family member is not a real decision. It is currently running at 50% off with a 90-day guarantee.</p>
`,
};

/* FAQ answers the five objections named in the offer's own creative research. */
