  },
  {
    q: "How is this different from a $15 LifeStraw?",
    a: "It is a fair question, and the LifeStraw is genuinely good — we have ranked it #2 rather than buried it. The basic LifeStraw is a straw: you drink directly from the water source, which means getting down to a stream, puddle or floodwater. Our top pick adds a squeeze pouch and a standard bottle adapter, so you can fill a container, carry it, and hand it to someone. If you are an adult preparing for yourself, the price difference is real and the LifeStraw is a sensible buy. If you are preparing for a household with children in it, the difference in how it gets used is the thing you are paying for.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is it safe for children to drink filtered creek or pond water?",
    a: "A 0.1 micron filter is designed to remove bacteria and parasites, which are the realistic risks in most US surface water and in a domestic supply after flooding or a boil notice. It does not remove viruses, heavy metals or chemical contamination. So: creek, pond, rain barrel or a tap under a boil notice, yes, that is what these are built for. Water near industrial runoff, agricultural chemicals or an unknown spill is a different problem that no personal filter solves, and the honest answer there is do not drink it.",
  },
  {
    q: "What if it breaks when I need it most?",
    a: "There is very little to break. There is no pump, no battery, no electronics and no moving part beyond the cap. That is the main practical argument for this type of filter over anything powered: the failure modes that take out other emergency gear, like flat batteries and no mains power, do not apply. Our top pick also carries a 90-day money-back guarantee, so the useful thing to do is buy it, try it on tap water this week, and confirm it works while nothing is going wrong.",
  },
  {
    q: "Will my kids actually use it in an emergency?",
    a: "This is the reason our #1 and #2 picks are ranked the way they are. A filter only helps if the person holding it can work it out while frightened and possibly in the dark. A squeeze pouch that fills like a water bottle needs no explanation. A bare straw that has to be used face-down at the water's edge is harder for a child to manage alone. If you buy nothing else from this page, buy the version your household can use without you standing over them.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do you rate these products?",
    a: "Our Insider Score weighs verified customer ratings (50%), review volume and consistency (20%), value for money (15%), and brand reliability and buyer protection (15%). For direct-sold products with no retailer review aggregate, we say so plainly and score on published specifications, design and guarantee instead. The letter grades and percentage bars are our editorial assessment, not laboratory measurements. See our full <a href='/how-we-rate/'>methodology</a>.",
  },
];
